1.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: Round 4 convergence

Full 5/5 unanimity now reached on an 18-champion set. All five colleagues in round 4 list exactly: Gragas, Vi, Fizz, Galio, Kayn, Orn, Poppy, Pyke, Rell, Riven, Tryndamere, Yone, Zac, Renekton, Nidalee, Fiora, Camille, BelVeth.

This matches the high-confidence ≥4/5 set from Round 3 and now upgrades it to 5/5. Core canonical travel/wind-up dashes with intrinsic damage/CC on arrival that is routinely angle-corrected/extended by a Flash buffer:

Core 8 present in all lists from Round 3 onward: Gragas E, Vi Q, Fizz E, Pyke E, Riven Q3, Tryndamere E, Yone Q3, Camille E.

High-confidence additions that have converged: Kayn Q, Orn/Ornn E, Poppy E, Rell W, Zac E, Renekton E, Nidalee W, Fiora Q, Galio E, BelVeth Q.

Criteria being applied consistently across all 5: Dash-Flash counts only when Flash can be buffered in the travel/wind-up window of the dash itself so the intrinsic damage and/or hard CC applied on arrival of that same dash is angle-corrected/extended and lands more reliably/more optimally. Reposition-only blinks, ally-targeted teleports, and Flash used only to enable a separate follow-up are excluded.

Excluded unanimously in Round 4 under the improve-the-dash’s-own-damage/CC-landing rule, not reposition-only or follow-up enable:
Jarvan E+Q – flash to enable follow-up Q Cataclysm, not to improve E’s own CC landing;
LeBlanc W Distortion – instant blink/reposition, no intrinsic dash damage/CC;
Shen E Shadow Dash – teleport to ally, reposition only;
Tristana W Rocket Jump – pure mobility/reposition;
Corki W Valkyrie – reposition mobility;
Sejuani Q Permafrost – skillshot charge/root, not classic dash buffer;
Rakan W Gleam – marginal / not consistently documented as improving the dash’s own intrinsic CC/damage landing under strict hitbox-follows-Flash test;
Urgot E Slide – marginal / not consistently documented as dash-landing optimisation.

Colleague 4 change: previously outlier strict test including Jarvan, Sejuani, Shen, Urgot and arguing for Riven Q3 = Yone Q3. In Round 4 Colleague 4 converges on majority stricter practical set, drops Jarvan/Sejuani/Shen/Urgot as disputed edge cases, and explicitly retains Riven Q3 together with Yone Q3 as same structural class: dash/leap into arrival knock-up hitbox shiftable by buffered Flash.

Naming note: Orn/Ornn variation persists; ability is E Tectonic Break.

Unresolved boundary questions now effectively closed by consensus: Rakan W and Urgot E remain out, Jarvan E+Q remains out. The 18-list is the working belief with high confidence.

